$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/ai-foundations/agile-scrum-user-stories

::agile-scrum-user-stories o1 - Sprint order::It is the last day of a sprint, and the Sprint Review with stakeholders has just finished. Which Scrum event comes next? {
=The Sprint Retrospective, where the team improves how it works #Correct. The Sprint Review inspects the result, then the Sprint Retrospective closes the sprint by improving how the team works.
~Sprint Planning for the next sprint #Not quite. Planning starts the next sprint, but only after the Sprint Retrospective ends this one.
~A Daily Scrum to adjust the plan #Not quite. Daily Scrums happen during the sprint; after the Review comes the Retrospective.
~Nothing, because the Sprint Review is the last event #Not quite. The Sprint Retrospective follows the Sprint Review and is the last event of the sprint.
}

::agile-scrum-user-stories o2 - User story form::A team wants learners to be able to download a course certificate. Which backlog item is written as a user story? {
=As a learner, I want to download my certificate, so that I can show it to an employer #Correct. It names a user role, something they can do and a benefit, and leaves out how it will be built.
~Add a PDF library and a download button to the profile page #Not quite. This says how to build it and names no user role or benefit.
~As a learner, I want the certificate built with a PDF library #Not quite. It has no so that benefit, and it describes how it will be built instead of what the learner can do.
~Given I finished the course, when I press Download, then a certificate file is saved #Not quite. Given, When, Then is a way to write an acceptance criterion, not the story itself.
}

::agile-scrum-user-stories o3 - Testable criterion::Story. As a learner, I want to search the topic list, so that I can find a topic quickly. Which acceptance criterion is testable? {
=Given the list has a topic named Linux permissions, when I type permissions in the search box, then that topic appears in the results #Correct. It names a situation, an action and an observable result, so a tester can answer yes or no.
~Search is fast and easy to use #Not quite. Fast and easy cannot be checked with a clear yes or no.
~Given I search, when results appear, then they feel relevant #Not quite. It has the Given, When, Then shape, but feel relevant is not an observable result.
~Search uses a single index so it stays quick #Not quite. This describes how it is built, not a yes or no check of what the learner sees.
}
